{"id":"ED_PRO-0140","dept":"ED_PRO","deptName":"ED — Professional","difficulty":"moderate","title":"Acute diverticulitis, admitted","patient":{"name":"Wendy Cross","sex":"F","age":62},"record":"EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT — CLINICAL NOTE\nPatient: Wendy Cross · F · Age 62   MRN: E294494   Acct: V72100140\nFacility: Riverside Medical Centre   DOS: 2026-06-06\nProvider: Dr. L. Palmer, MD (Emergency Medicine)\n\nCHIEF COMPLAINT: Left lower abdominal pain and fever.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS:\n62 y.o. woman with 2 days of left iliac-fossa pain, fever and change in bowel habit, worsening, tolerating little orally. No rectal bleeding.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: Positive for LIF pain, fever, altered bowels. Negative for rectal bleeding, vomiting all intake. Others negative.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY: Diverticulosis.   MEDICATIONS: None.   ALLERGIES: NKDA.\n\nEXAMINATION:\nVitals: T 38.3C, HR 96, BP 126/78, RR 18, SpO2 98%.\nLeft iliac-fossa tenderness with localised guarding, no generalised peritonism, no palpable mass.\n\nMEDICAL DECISION MAKING:\nProblems: one acute illness with systemic features. Data reviewed: FBC/CRP, urinalysis, CT abdomen (acute sigmoid diverticulitis, no perforation/abscess). Risk: moderate — IV antibiotics, analgesia, admission.\n\nASSESSMENT & PLAN:\n1. Acute diverticulitis. IV antibiotics, analgesia, bowel rest, admission, surgical review if deterioration. Moderate complexity.","answerKey":[{"code":"K57.32","desc":"Diverticulitis of large intestine without perforation or abscess without bleeding","type":"PDx","why":"CT-confirmed acute sigmoid diverticulitis without perforation, abscess or bleeding supports K57.32."},{"code":"99284","desc":"ED visit, moderate MDM (Level 4)","type":"CPT","why":"Acute systemic illness, multiple data (bloods, CT), moderate-risk IV treatment and admission — supports Level 4."}]},
{"id":"ED_PRO-0141","dept":"ED_PRO","deptName":"ED — Professional","difficulty":"moderate","title":"Acute vertigo, central excluded","patient":{"name":"Alan Grant","sex":"M","age":55},"record":"EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT — CLINICAL NOTE\nPatient: Alan Grant · M · Age 55   MRN: E418614   Acct: V72100141\nFacility: Riverside Medical Centre   DOS: 2026-07-07\nProvider: Dr. R. Walker, MD (Emergency Medicine)\n\nCHIEF COMPLAINT: Persistent spinning with vomiting.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS:\n55 y.o. man with persistent spinning vertigo and vomiting for hours, worse on movement, no hearing loss, no diplopia, no limb weakness, hypertensive.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: Positive for vertigo, vomiting. Negative for focal weakness, diplopia, hearing loss. Others negative.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY: Hypertension.   MEDICATIONS: Amlodipine.   ALLERGIES: NKDA.\n\nEXAMINATION:\nVitals: T 36.8C, HR 84, BP 150/90, RR 16, SpO2 98%.\nHorizontal nystagmus, reassuring HINTS features (favouring peripheral), unsteady but no other focal neurology, normal coordination and power.\n\nMEDICAL DECISION MAKING:\nProblems: one acute illness with uncertain prognosis (peripheral vs central vertigo, vascular risk). Data reviewed: bedside HINTS, glucose, imaging considered per red flags. Risk: moderate — antiemetic, central-cause exclusion, follow-up.\n\nASSESSMENT & PLAN:\n1. Acute peripheral vestibular vertigo, central cause excluded clinically. Antiemetic, vestibular sedative short course, safety-net for central features. Moderate complexity.","answerKey":[{"code":"H81.399","desc":"Other peripheral vertigo, unspecified ear","type":"PDx","why":"Peripheral vestibular features with reassuring examination support a peripheral-vertigo code."},{"code":"99284","desc":"ED visit, moderate MDM (Level 4)","type":"CPT","why":"Acute problem with uncertain prognosis requiring central-cause exclusion in a vascular-risk patient, assessment/data reviewed, moderate risk — supports Level 4."}]},
{"id":"ED_PRO-0142","dept":"ED_PRO","deptName":"ED — Professional","difficulty":"moderate","title":"Acute cholecystitis with systemic upset","patient":{"name":"Paula Nash","sex":"F","age":48},"record":"EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT — CLINICAL NOTE\nPatient: Paula Nash · F · Age 48   MRN: E793977   Acct: V72100142\nFacility: Riverside Medical Centre   DOS: 2026-08-08\nProvider: Dr. J. Harris, MD (Emergency Medicine)\n\nCHIEF COMPLAINT: RUQ pain, fever, vomiting.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS:\n48 y.o. woman with a day of constant right upper quadrant pain, fever and vomiting, known gallstones, worse than prior episodes. No jaundice.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: Positive for RUQ pain, fever, vomiting. Negative for jaundice, chest pain. Others negative.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY: Gallstones.   MEDICATIONS: None.   ALLERGIES: NKDA.\n\nEXAMINATION:\nVitals: T 38.4C, HR 100, BP 124/78, RR 18, SpO2 98%.\nRUQ tenderness with a positive Murphy's sign, mild guarding, no generalised peritonism, no jaundice.\n\nMEDICAL DECISION MAKING:\nProblems: one acute illness with systemic features. Data reviewed: FBC/CRP/LFTs, ultrasound (wall thickening, pericholecystic fluid, stones). Risk: moderate — IV antibiotics, analgesia, admission and surgical referral.\n\nASSESSMENT & PLAN:\n1. Acute cholecystitis. IV antibiotics, analgesia, nil by mouth, surgical referral, admission. Moderate complexity.","answerKey":[{"code":"K81.0","desc":"Acute cholecystitis","type":"PDx","why":"Positive Murphy's sign with ultrasound inflammatory changes supports acute cholecystitis."},{"code":"99284","desc":"ED visit, moderate MDM (Level 4)","type":"CPT","why":"Acute systemic illness, multiple data (bloods, ultrasound), moderate-risk treatment and admission — supports Level 4."}]},
{"id":"ED_PRO-0143","dept":"ED_PRO","deptName":"ED — Professional","difficulty":"advanced","title":"Massive haematemesis with shock","patient":{"name":"Bruce Dean","sex":"M","age":70},"record":"EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT — CLINICAL NOTE\nPatient: Bruce Dean · M · Age 70   MRN: E765419   Acct: V72100143\nFacility: Riverside Medical Centre   DOS: 2026-09-09\nProvider: Dr. K. Lewis, MD (Emergency Medicine)\n\nCHIEF COMPLAINT: Vomiting large volumes of blood.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS:\n70 y.o. man vomiting large volumes of fresh blood, feeling faint, known peptic ulcer disease on regular NSAIDs. Pale and clammy on arrival.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: Positive for haematemesis, faintness. Negative for chest pain, focal deficit. Limited by state.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY: Peptic ulcer disease.   MEDICATIONS: NSAIDs.   ALLERGIES: NKDA.\n\nEXAMINATION:\nVitals: HR 124, BP 86/52, RR 24, SpO2 96%, T 36.6C.\nPale, clammy, tachycardic and hypotensive, epigastric tenderness, melaena on rectal exam.\n\nMEDICAL DECISION MAKING:\nProblems: one acute illness posing a threat to life (major upper GI bleed with shock). Data (extensive): FBC, coagulation, cross-match, U&E/LFTs, venous gas/lactate. Risk: high — major-haemorrhage protocol, blood products, IV PPI, urgent endoscopy.\n\nASSESSMENT & PLAN:\n1. Major upper GI haemorrhage with shock (bleeding peptic ulcer). Resuscitation with blood products, IV PPI, urgent endoscopy, critical-care/admission. High complexity, life-threatening.","answerKey":[{"code":"K92.2","desc":"Gastrointestinal haemorrhage, unspecified","type":"PDx","why":"Major GI haemorrhage with shock, source pending endoscopy, is coded as GI haemorrhage as the condition responsible for admission."},{"code":"99285","desc":"ED visit, high MDM (Level 5)","type":"CPT","why":"Threat-to-life haemorrhage with shock, extensive data, high-risk resuscitation with blood products — meets High."}]},
{"id":"ED_PRO-0144","dept":"ED_PRO","deptName":"ED — Professional","difficulty":"advanced","title":"Acute pulmonary oedema","patient":{"name":"Alison Frost","sex":"F","age":55},"record":"EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT — CLINICAL NOTE\nPatient: Alison Frost · F · Age 55   MRN: E404497   Acct: V72100144\nFacility: Riverside Medical Centre   DOS: 2026-01-10\nProvider: Dr. M. Coleman, MD (Emergency Medicine)\n\nCHIEF COMPLAINT: Severe breathlessness, unable to lie flat.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS:\n55 y.o. woman with sudden severe breathlessness, unable to lie flat, frothy sputum, known heart failure. Distressed and hypoxic on arrival.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: Positive for severe dyspnoea, orthopnoea, frothy sputum. Negative for chest pain (limited), fever. Others negative.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY: Heart failure.   MEDICATIONS: Furosemide.   ALLERGIES: NKDA.\n\nEXAMINATION:\nVitals: HR 118, BP 176/104, RR 30, SpO2 86% RA, T 36.9C.\nSevere respiratory distress, widespread crackles and wheeze, raised JVP, sweaty and anxious, using accessory muscles.\n\nMEDICAL DECISION MAKING:\nProblems: one acute illness posing a threat to life (acute pulmonary oedema). Data (extensive): ECG, CXR (congestion), gases, troponin/BNP, U&E. Risk: high — sit up, high-flow oxygen/CPAP, IV nitrates and diuretics, close monitoring, critical-care involvement.\n\nASSESSMENT & PLAN:\n1. Acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema. Oxygen/CPAP, IV nitrates and diuretics, monitoring, treat precipitant, critical-care referral and admission. High complexity, life-threatening.","answerKey":[{"code":"I50.1","desc":"Left ventricular failure, unspecified","type":"PDx","why":"Acute pulmonary oedema from left ventricular failure supports the left-ventricular-failure code as the acute condition."},{"code":"99285","desc":"ED visit, high MDM (Level 5)","type":"CPT","why":"Threat-to-life presentation, extensive data, high-risk treatment (CPAP, IV nitrates/diuretics) with monitoring — meets High."},{"code":"93010","desc":"ECG, interpretation and report only","type":"CPT","why":"ECG interpreted by the ED provider — separately reportable."}]},
{"id":"ED_PRO-0145","dept":"ED_PRO","deptName":"ED — Professional","difficulty":"advanced","title":"Necrotising soft tissue infection suspected","patient":{"name":"Carl Ellis","sex":"M","age":38},"record":"EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT — CLINICAL NOTE\nPatient: Carl Ellis · M · Age 38   MRN: E440302   Acct: V72100145\nFacility: Riverside Medical Centre   DOS: 2026-02-11\nProvider: Dr. P. Grant, MD (Emergency Medicine)\n\nCHIEF COMPLAINT: Rapidly spreading painful leg with systemic upset.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS:\n38 y.o. man with rapidly spreading severe leg pain, redness and swelling over hours with pain out of proportion, fever and feeling very unwell. Diabetic.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: Positive for severe leg pain, fever, systemic upset. Negative for trauma initially recalled. Limited by distress.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY: T2DM.   MEDICATIONS: Metformin.   ALLERGIES: NKDA.\n\nEXAMINATION:\nVitals: T 39.0C, HR 122, BP 96/58, RR 24, SpO2 96%.\nTense swollen erythematous leg with pain out of proportion, areas of dusky skin and blistering, crepitus in places, systemically unwell/hypotensive — suspected necrotising fasciitis.\n\nMEDICAL DECISION MAKING:\nProblems: one acute illness posing a threat to life and limb (necrotising soft-tissue infection). Data (extensive): FBC/CRP/U&E/lactate/CK, cultures, imaging not to delay surgery. Risk: high — resuscitation, broad-spectrum IV antibiotics, immediate surgical referral for debridement.\n\nASSESSMENT & PLAN:\n1. Suspected necrotising fasciitis with sepsis. Aggressive resuscitation, broad-spectrum IV antibiotics, immediate surgical referral for exploration/debridement, critical-care. High complexity, life- and limb-threatening.","answerKey":[{"code":"M72.6","desc":"Necrotizing fasciitis","type":"PDx","why":"Rapidly spreading infection with pain out of proportion, dusky skin and crepitus supports necrotising fasciitis."},{"code":"99285","desc":"ED visit, high MDM (Level 5)","type":"CPT","why":"Threat to life and limb, extensive data, high-risk resuscitation and immediate surgery — meets High."}]},
{"id":"ED_PRO-0146","dept":"ED_PRO","deptName":"ED — Professional","difficulty":"advanced","title":"Acute mesenteric ischaemia suspected","patient":{"name":"Bethany Wells","sex":"F","age":68},"record":"EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT — CLINICAL NOTE\nPatient: Bethany Wells · F · Age 68   MRN: E645716   Acct: V72100146\nFacility: Riverside Medical Centre   DOS: 2026-03-12\nProvider: Dr. S. Reed, MD (Emergency Medicine)\n\nCHIEF COMPLAINT: Severe abdominal pain out of proportion.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS:\n68 y.o. woman with sudden severe central abdominal pain out of proportion to examination, vomiting, known atrial fibrillation and not anticoagulated. Becoming systemically unwell.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: Positive for severe abdominal pain, vomiting. Negative for chest pain, urinary symptoms. Others negative.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY: Atrial fibrillation.   MEDICATIONS: Not anticoagulated.   ALLERGIES: NKDA.\n\nEXAMINATION:\nVitals: HR 112 irregular, BP 104/64, RR 22, SpO2 97%, T 37.4C.\nSevere pain with a relatively soft abdomen early (pain out of proportion), later developing tenderness, systemically unwell.\n\nMEDICAL DECISION MAKING:\nProblems: one acute illness posing a threat to life (mesenteric ischaemia). Data (extensive): lactate (raised), FBC/U&E, venous gas, CT angiography of the mesenteric vessels, ECG (AF). Risk: high — resuscitation, urgent surgical/vascular referral, anticoagulation consideration.\n\nASSESSMENT & PLAN:\n1. Suspected acute mesenteric ischaemia (embolic, AF). Resuscitation, urgent CT angiography, surgical/vascular referral, analgesia, critical-care. High complexity, life-threatening.","answerKey":[{"code":"K55.019","desc":"Acute (reversible) ischemia of small intestine, extent unspecified","type":"PDx","why":"Pain out of proportion with raised lactate and an embolic source (AF) supports acute vascular disorder of the intestine (mesenteric ischaemia)."},{"code":"99285","desc":"ED visit, high MDM (Level 5)","type":"CPT","why":"Threat-to-life problem, extensive data, high-risk urgent surgical pathway — meets High."}]},
{"id":"ED_PRO-0147","dept":"ED_PRO","deptName":"ED — Professional","difficulty":"advanced","title":"Severe head injury","patient":{"name":"Dean Morris","sex":"M","age":25},"record":"EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT — CLINICAL NOTE\nPatient: Dean Morris · M · Age 25   MRN: E281623   Acct: V72100147\nFacility: Riverside Medical Centre   DOS: 2026-04-13\nProvider: Dr. T. Brooks, MD (Emergency Medicine)\n\nCHIEF COMPLAINT: Assault, reduced consciousness.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS:\n25 y.o. man assaulted with a head injury, initial reduced consciousness, now drowsy and confused with vomiting, brought in by ambulance with cervical precautions.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: Positive for head injury, reduced consciousness, vomiting. Limited by conscious level.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY: Unknown.   MEDICATIONS: Unknown.   ALLERGIES: Unknown.\n\nEXAMINATION:\nVitals: GCS 12 (E3 V4 M5), HR 88, BP 148/88, RR 16, SpO2 98%, pupils being monitored.\nDrowsy and confused, scalp haematoma, pupils currently equal and reactive, cervical spine immobilised, moving all four limbs, no other obvious major injury on primary survey.\n\nMEDICAL DECISION MAKING:\nProblems: one acute injury posing a threat to life (significant head injury with reduced GCS). Data (extensive): urgent CT head and cervical spine, glucose, bloods, coagulation. Risk: high — airway monitoring, neuroprotection, neurosurgical liaison.\n\nASSESSMENT & PLAN:\n1. Significant traumatic brain injury with reduced GCS. Urgent CT head/C-spine, neuroprotective measures, neurosurgical referral, close neuro-observation, admission. High complexity, life-threatening.","answerKey":[{"code":"S06.9X1A","desc":"Unspecified intracranial injury with loss of consciousness of 30 minutes or less, initial","type":"PDx","why":"Significant head injury with reduced consciousness at initial encounter supports an intracranial-injury code per documentation and imaging."},{"code":"99285","desc":"ED visit, high MDM (Level 5)","type":"CPT","why":"Threat-to-life injury with reduced GCS, extensive urgent data, high-risk management — meets High."}]},
{"id":"ED_PRO-0148","dept":"ED_PRO","deptName":"ED — Professional","difficulty":"advanced","title":"Massive obstetric haemorrhage risk / ruptured ectopic","patient":{"name":"Naomi Clark","sex":"F","age":30},"record":"EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT — CLINICAL NOTE\nPatient: Naomi Clark · F · Age 30   MRN: E869272   Acct: V72100148\nFacility: Riverside Medical Centre   DOS: 2026-05-14\nProvider: Dr. L. Palmer, MD (Emergency Medicine)\n\nCHIEF COMPLAINT: Collapse with abdominal pain, positive pregnancy test.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS:\n30 y.o. woman with sudden severe lower abdominal pain and collapse, positive pregnancy test, LMP about 7 weeks ago. Pale and clammy, shoulder-tip pain reported.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: Positive for severe abdominal pain, collapse, shoulder-tip pain. Limited by state.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY: Nil.   MEDICATIONS: None.   ALLERGIES: NKDA.\n\nEXAMINATION:\nVitals: HR 122, BP 84/50, RR 24, SpO2 98%, T 36.6C.\nPale, clammy, hypotensive, generalised abdominal tenderness with peritonism, features of intra-abdominal haemorrhage — suspected ruptured ectopic pregnancy.\n\nMEDICAL DECISION MAKING:\nProblems: one acute illness posing an immediate threat to life (ruptured ectopic with shock). Data: beta-hCG, FBC, cross-match, bedside ultrasound (free fluid); do not delay resuscitation/surgery. Risk: high — major-haemorrhage protocol, immediate gynaecology/surgical referral for theatre.\n\nASSESSMENT & PLAN:\n1. Suspected ruptured ectopic pregnancy with haemorrhagic shock. Resuscitation with blood products, immediate gynaecology referral for emergency surgery, cross-match, admission to theatre. High complexity, life-threatening.","answerKey":[{"code":"O00.101","desc":"Ruptured tubal pregnancy, right side","type":"PDx","why":"Collapse with peritonism and free fluid in early pregnancy supports a ruptured ectopic (tubal) pregnancy per documentation."},{"code":"99285","desc":"ED visit, high MDM (Level 5)","type":"CPT","why":"Immediate life threat with haemorrhagic shock requiring emergent surgery — meets High."}]},
{"id":"ED_PRO-0149","dept":"ED_PRO","deptName":"ED — Professional","difficulty":"advanced","title":"Acute cauda equina syndrome","patient":{"name":"Frank Hale","sex":"M","age":58},"record":"EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT — CLINICAL NOTE\nPatient: Frank Hale · M · Age 58   MRN: E325760   Acct: V72100149\nFacility: Riverside Medical Centre   DOS: 2026-06-15\nProvider: Dr. R. Walker, MD (Emergency Medicine)\n\nCHIEF COMPLAINT: Back pain with leg weakness and urinary retention.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS:\n58 y.o. man with severe back pain now with bilateral leg weakness, numbness around the perineum and difficulty passing urine over hours. Red-flag features present.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: Positive for back pain, leg weakness, saddle numbness, urinary difficulty. Negative for fever, trauma. Others negative.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY: Chronic back pain.   MEDICATIONS: Analgesia.   ALLERGIES: NKDA.\n\nEXAMINATION:\nVitals: T 36.8C, HR 84, BP 138/84, RR 16, SpO2 98%.\nBilateral lower-limb weakness, reduced perianal (saddle) sensation, reduced anal tone, palpable distended bladder — features of cauda equina syndrome.\n\nMEDICAL DECISION MAKING:\nProblems: one acute illness posing a threat to bodily function (cauda equina syndrome). Data (extensive): focused neuro exam, bladder scan (retention), urgent MRI arranged. Risk: high — emergency imaging and neurosurgical/spinal referral for time-critical decompression.\n\nASSESSMENT & PLAN:\n1. Cauda equina syndrome. Urgent MRI, immediate spinal/neurosurgical referral for emergency decompression, catheterisation, admission. High complexity, threat to bodily function.","answerKey":[{"code":"G83.4","desc":"Cauda equina syndrome","type":"PDx","why":"Saddle anaesthesia, bilateral leg weakness, reduced anal tone and retention support cauda equina syndrome."},{"code":"99285","desc":"ED visit, high MDM (Level 5)","type":"CPT","why":"Threat to bodily function requiring emergency imaging and surgery, extensive data, high-risk time-critical management — meets High."},{"code":"51702","desc":"Insertion of temporary indwelling bladder catheter, simple","type":"CPT","why":"Urethral catheterisation for retention performed — separately reportable."}]},
{"id":"ED_PRO-0150","dept":"ED_PRO","deptName":"ED — Professional","difficulty":"advanced","title":"Acute severe hyponatraemia with seizure","patient":{"name":"Emily Carter","sex":"F","age":63},"record":"EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT — CLINICAL NOTE\nPatient: Emily Carter · F · Age 63   MRN: E866104   Acct: V72100150\nFacility: Riverside Medical Centre   DOS: 2026-07-16\nProvider: Dr. J. Harris, MD (Emergency Medicine)\n\nCHIEF COMPLAINT: Confusion and a seizure, very low sodium.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS:\n63 y.o. woman with progressive confusion and then a seizure, on thiazide diuretic, found to have a very low sodium. Post-ictal on arrival.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: Positive for confusion, seizure (resolved). Negative for focal deficit now, fever. Limited history.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY: Hypertension.   MEDICATIONS: Thiazide diuretic.   ALLERGIES: NKDA.\n\nEXAMINATION:\nVitals: T 36.9C, HR 92, BP 138/84, RR 16, SpO2 98%, GCS improving post-ictally.\nDrowsy post-ictally, no focal neurology, no neck stiffness, no injuries of concern.\n\nMEDICAL DECISION MAKING:\nProblems: one acute illness posing a threat to life (severe symptomatic hyponatraemia with seizure). Data (extensive): U&E (severe hyponatraemia), serum/urine osmolality, glucose, venous gas, CT head as indicated. Risk: high — careful hypertonic saline for the seizing patient with close monitoring to avoid overcorrection, critical-care involvement.\n\nASSESSMENT & PLAN:\n1. Severe symptomatic hyponatraemia with seizure. Cautious hypertonic saline with strict sodium monitoring, treat the cause (stop thiazide), critical-care admission. High complexity, life-threatening.","answerKey":[{"code":"E87.1","desc":"Hypo-osmolality and hyponatremia","type":"PDx","why":"Severe symptomatic hyponatraemia causing seizure supports the hyponatraemia code as the emergency being treated."},{"code":"99285","desc":"ED visit, high MDM (Level 5)","type":"CPT","why":"Threat-to-life electrolyte emergency with seizure, extensive data, high-risk treatment with close monitoring — meets High."}]}
